Conceptually:

LifecycleManager
LifecyclePolicy
LifecycleTransitionValidator

The component should provide operations equivalent to:

canTransition()
validateTransition()
transition()

Use the existing project naming conventions.

Transition results must be deterministic.

Do not mutate input objects.

============================================================
4. DETERMINISTIC CLOCK
============================================================

Do NOT use Date.now() directly throughout business logic.

Create/use a clock abstraction.

Conceptually:

Clock
  getNow(): Date

Production implementation:

SystemClock

Test implementation:

FixedClock

This is required so TTL and expiration tests are deterministic.

Do not introduce fake timers unless they are actually useful.

============================================================
5. TTL EVALUATION
============================================================

Implement TTL evaluation according to the architecture.

Support:

- explicit TTL
- per-memory-type default TTL
- no-expiration cases if allowed
- expiresAt
- expiration detection

Sprint 1 already fixed default TTL behavior.

Preserve that behavior.

Example:

Conversation → configured default TTL
Temporary → configured default TTL

Do not hard-code these values.

Always derive from validated configuration.

============================================================
6. EXPIRATION
============================================================

Implement deterministic expiration evaluation.

Conceptually:

isExpired(memory, now)

A memory is expired only when its expiration policy says so.

Handle:

- exact expiration boundary
- before expiration
- after expiration
- missing expiration
- invalid expiration
- already expired memory
- archived memory
- deleted memory

Do not allow deleted memories to become active again.

============================================================
7. RETENTION POLICY
============================================================

Implement retention evaluation based on the architecture.

Support the configured concepts for:

- retention duration
- retention class/policy
- expiration
- archival
- deletion eligibility

Do not invent business rules.

If the architecture leaves a rule unresolved, document it as deferred
rather than guessing.

============================================================
8. ARCHIVAL
============================================================

Implement archival eligibility.

Archiving must:

- validate lifecycle transition
- preserve memory identity
- preserve ownership
- preserve security classification
- preserve audit-relevant metadata
- increment/update lifecycle metadata as required
- avoid exposing content in logs

Archiving must not automatically delete the memory.

============================================================
9. DELETION
============================================================

Implement deletion eligibility and lifecycle transition.

Deletion must enforce:

- lifecycle rules
- retention rules
- authorization contract
- version safety where applicable

Do not physically delete from a production database because no
production database exists yet.

The repository abstraction should be used.

If Sprint 1 defines logical deletion, preserve that model.

Do not redesign persistence.

============================================================
10. RETENTION DECISION
============================================================

Create a deterministic decision result for lifecycle evaluation.

Conceptually:

RetentionDecision:

- KEEP
- EXPIRE
- ARCHIVE
- DELETE

Use exact project terminology if already defined.

The result should explain the decision through safe structured metadata.

Do not include memory content in diagnostic details.

